          },
          {
            "section_type": "example_explanation",
            "normalized_html": "<p>To add two fractions with like denominators, we add the numerators and keep the denominators the same. Therefore,</p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: (3)/(8) + (1)/(8) = (3 + 1)/(8) = (4)/(8).]</span></p><p>We can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and the denominator by <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 4{:}]</span></p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\dfrac{4}{8} = \\dfrac{4\\div 4}{8\\div 4} = \\dfrac{1}{2}]</span></p><p>Therefore, we conclude that </p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\dfrac 3 8 + \\dfrac 1 8 = \\dfrac 1 2.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "To add two fractions with like denominators, we add the numerators and keep the denominators the same. Therefore,\n\n[MATH: (3)/(8) + (1)/(8) = (3 + 1)/(8) = (4)/(8).]\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and the denominator by [MATH: 4{:}]\n\n[MATH: \\dfrac{4}{8} = \\dfrac{4\\div 4}{8\\div 4} = \\dfrac{1}{2}]\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n[MATH: \\dfrac 3 8 + \\dfrac 1 8 = \\dfrac 1 2.]",
