    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nWhen adding two fractions with the same denominator, we\n\nContent:\n- add their numerators, and\n- keep the denominator the same.\n\nContent:\nFor example, let's compute the following sum:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\dfrac 1 3 + \\dfrac 1 3]\n\nContent:\nTo add these numbers, we add the numerators and keep the denominators the same. Therefore,\n\nContent:\n[MATH: (1)/(3) + (1)/(3) = (1 + 1)/(3) = (2)/(3).]\n\nContent:\nWe can use a fraction model to confirm this result:\n\nGraphic:\n[IMG: /graphics/dd4276d10ec964b141f90dd9cfbc0d6e]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Adding Fractions\n\nExample Prompt:\nFind the value of [MATH: \\dfrac 2 9 + \\dfrac 3 9.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTo add two fractions with like denominators, we add the numerators and keep the denominators the same. Therefore,\n\n[MATH: (2)/(9) + (3)/(9) = (2 + 3)/(9) = (5)/(9).]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (3)/(7) + (2)/(7) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (6)/(7)]\nb. [MATH: (4)/(7)]\nc. [MATH: (2)/(7)]\nd. [MATH: (5)/(14)]\ne. [MATH: (5)/(7)]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (4)/(7) + (5)/(7) =][FREE_ENTRY 1]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Adding Fractions and Simplifying the Result\n\nExample Prompt:\nWhat is the value of [MATH: \\dfrac 3 8 + \\dfrac 1 8 ?]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTo add two fractions with like denominators, we add the numerators and keep the denominators the same. Therefore,\n\n[MATH: (3)/(8) + (1)/(8) = (3 + 1)/(8) = (4)/(8).]\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and the denominator by [MATH: 4{:}]\n\n[MATH: \\dfrac{4}{8} = \\dfrac{4\\div 4}{8\\div 4} = \\dfrac{1}{2}]\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n[MATH: \\dfrac 3 8 + \\dfrac 1 8 = \\dfrac 1 2.]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (1)/(4) + (1)/(4) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (1)/(4)]\nb. [MATH: (1)/(2)]\nc. [MATH: (1)/(8)]\nd. [MATH: (1)/(16)]\ne. [MATH: (2)/(8)]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\nExpressed as a fraction in lowest terms,[MATH: (4)/(12) + (5)/(12) =]\n[FREE_ENTRY 1].\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Adding Fractions and Converting the Result to a Mixed Number\n\nExample Prompt:\nFind the value of [MATH: \\dfrac 2 3 + \\dfrac 2 3.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTo add two fractions with like denominators, we add the numerators and keep the denominators the same. Therefore,\n\n[MATH: (2)/(3) + (2)/(3) = (2 + 2)/(3) = (4)/(3).]\n\nWe then convert [MATH: \\dfrac 4 3] to a mixed number:\n\n[MATH: 4\\div 3 = 1\\,\\textrm{R}1 = 1\\,\\dfrac 1 3]\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n[MATH: \\dfrac 2 3 + \\dfrac 2 3 = 1\\,\\dfrac 1 3.]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (3)/(5) + (3)/(5) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (6)/(10)]\nb. [MATH: 1(2)/(5)]\nc. [MATH: 1]\nd. [MATH: 1(1)/(5)]\ne. [MATH: (1)/(5)]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\nExpressed as a mixed number in lowest terms,[MATH: (5)/(8) + (7)/(8) =]\n[FREE_ENTRY 1]."
